RETURN f;
13. E-Commerce Graph
Create nodes
CREATE (c:Customer {id:1,name:'Rahul'}),
       (p:Product {id:101,name:'Laptop'}),
       (cat:Category {name:'Electronics'}),
       (o:Order {id:5001});
Create relationships
MATCH (c:Customer {id:1}),
      (p:Product {id:101}),
      (cat:Category {name:'Electronics'}),
      (o:Order {id:5001})
CREATE (c)-[:PLACED]->(o),
       (o)-[:CONTAINS]->(p),
       (p)-[:BELONGS_TO]->(cat);
Find products purchased by Rahul
MATCH (c:Customer {name:'Rahul'})
      -[:PLACED]->(:Order)
      -[:CONTAINS]->(p:Product)
RETURN p;
14. Transactions, Consistency and Availability

Neo4j supports transactional operations.

Example:

CREATE (s:Student {id:101,name:'Rahul'});
MATCH
MATCH (s:Student {id:101})
RETURN s;
SET
MATCH (s:Student {id:101})
SET s.name='Rahul Kumar'
RETURN s;
DELETE
MATCH (s:Student {id:101})
DELETE s;

Multiple related operations can be executed within a transaction so that they are committed together or rolled back if an error occurs.

Consistency

Transactions help ensure that graph changes leave the database in a valid state.

Availability

Neo4j deployments can use clustering and replication for high availability. The exact capabilities depend on the Neo4j edition and deployment architecture.

15. Small Neo4j Application Using Python
Install Python Driver
pip install neo4j
Python Program
from neo4j import GraphDatabase

uri = "bolt://localhost:7687"
driver = GraphDatabase.driver(uri, auth=("neo4j", "password"))

def create_user(name):
    with driver.session() as s:
        s.run("CREATE (:User {name:$name})", name=name)

def get_users():
    with driver.session() as s:
        result = s.run("MATCH (u:User) RETURN u.name AS name")
        for r in result:
            print(r["name"])

create_user("Rahul")
create_user("Amit")
get_users()

driver.close()
Create relationship
with driver.session() as s:
    s.run("""
    MATCH (a:User {name:'Rahul'}),
